# Braid is already an Arrow. Control.Arrow's interface is not a library
# here — it is the syntax:
#
#   arr f      = any pure program        f >>> g   = f ; g
#   first f    = f _                     second f  = _ f
#   f *** g    = f g   (one stage!)      f &&& g   = dup ; f g
#   left f     = (f |)                   right f   = (| f)
#   f +++ g    = (f | g)                 f ||| g   = (f | g) ; merge
#   app        = ev
#
# The laws run below — with sq = square, db = double, on the pair 3 7.

def sq = dup ; *
def db = 2 _ ; *
def pair = (a b -> (a ; toStr) " " (b ; toStr) ; cat ... ; cat ; print)

# interchange: first f ; second g  =  second g ; first f  =  f *** g
# (the law premonoidal effects would break — pure Braid keeps it)
3 7 ; (sq _) ; (_ db) ; pair       # 9 14
3 7 ; (_ db) ; (sq _) ; pair       # 9 14
3 7 ; sq db   ; pair               # 9 14

# first is functorial: first (sq ; db) = first f ; first g
3 7 ; (sq ; db) _     ; pair       # 18 7
3 7 ; (sq _) ; (db _) ; pair       # 18 7

# naturality of fst (fst = id drop): first f ; fst = fst ; f
3 7 ; (sq _) ; id drop ; print    # 9
3 7 ; id drop ; sq     ; print    # 9

# ArrowChoice and ArrowApply
5 ; alt1 ; (sq | db) ; merge ; print    # 25 — f ||| g on the left track
[sq] 6 ; ev ; print                # 36 — app

# ---- and the interface itself (2026-09-09) ---------------------------
# Everything above spells the Arrow OPERATIONS out of Braid's syntax, at
# Braid's own category. `examples/circuits.braid` now declares the
# INTERFACE once, as `theory Arrow(k(_, _))` — a theory parameter that
# is a type constructor — and audits two models against it: circuits,
# and plain functions. `k` is not a higher-kinded type variable: it
# never reaches inference, and the instance head substitutes a declared
# data type for it before any slot is checked.
#
# `Fn` cannot be that data type. It is built in and it takes an ARROW,
# not two wires, so `instance F : Arrow(Fn)` is refused —
#   instance F: theory Arrow declares 'k' as a type constructor of
#   arity 2, so its argument names a declared data type; 'Fn' is not
#   one (`Fn` is built in and takes an arrow, not wires; wrap it —
#   `data Arr(a, b) = Fn⟨a ⇒ b⟩` — to name it here)
# — and the wrapper the message suggests is exactly what circuits.braid
# uses for its second model.
